            <div xml:id="d191">

                <pb facs="0204" n="192" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg192"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00/1682: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Kennedy</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-06-08T11:00:00+01:00">June 8, 1939—11
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 8—4:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>800. Personal for the Secretary of State. Halifax told me last night that the
                    British Minister at the Vatican had been told by the Pope that the Germans and
                    Italians had definitely told him war was absolutely inevitable and they saw no
                    means of avoiding it. Halifax thinks this of course may be another part of the
                    nerve-breaking pressure the Germans and Italians are using.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kennedy</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
